<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khara Khula</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1634">1634</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khara Khula was a chief of the Choros tribe of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirats</a> who built up a power base in the steppe between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai Mountains</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ili-river">Ili River</a> in the early seventeenth century. Leading Oirat resistance against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha Mongols</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altan-khan">Altan Khan</a> line, he laid the foundations of the Dzungar Khanate, which his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erdeni-batur">Erdeni Batur</a> organised after his death in 1634.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>At the start of the 17th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirat</a> tribes were roaming the pastures between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irtysh">Irtysh</a> and the Ili rivers, pushed west from their home in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai Mountains</a> by Ubashi Khong Tayiji, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha</a> prince who ruled the Altan Khanate. The Choros, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorbet">Dörbet</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torghut">Torghut</a>, Khoshut, Khoit and other tribes each had a hereditary ruler and competed with one another, a disunity that had lasted 150 years, since the Dörben Oirat state dissolved after the murder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esen-taishi">Esen Taishi</a> in 1454.</p><p>Khara Khula was born Khutugaitu and succeeded his father, Bulai (or Abuda Ablai Taishi), as taishi of the Choros around 1600. His father gave him the nickname by which historians know him: the name of a dark, lion-like animal of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai Mountains</a> that he was said to have killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From his base on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irtysh">Irtysh</a> and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarbagatai-mountains">Tarbagatai Mountains</a> he set out to build the unity that his ancestor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esen-taishi">Esen Taishi</a> had once demanded of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirat</a> princes. In 1606 he unified the tribes, in 1608 the Oirats defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a> to the west, and by 1609 he had won a decisive victory over Ubashi Khong Tayiji, forcing the Altan Khanate out of the Kobdo region. Some princes then returned to their independent ways. In 1610 he took the salt mines near the Russian outpost at Tara, provoking clashes with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> that lasted until 1613.</p><p>A severe winter in 1614 killed much of the livestock, and in 1615 Ubashi Khong Tayiji won a major victory. Some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirat</a> princes became his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, while others fled to southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> under Russian protection or to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a>. Defeats continued into the mid-1620s, until Khara Khula's counter-attacks culminated in Ubashi's death around 1627, after which he regained the lost Oirat lands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His growing dominance troubled the other princes, above all the leaders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torghut">Torghut</a>, who migrated from southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> to the pastures of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> to keep their freedom. Their departure eased the pressure on the pastures of southern Siberia and helped his son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erdeni-batur">Erdeni Batur</a>, to forge the remaining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirat</a> tribes into the Dzungar Khanate shortly after Khara Khula's death in 1634.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kharkhul” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Khara Khula</b>, chief of the Choros, built up a power base in the steppe between the Altai and the Ili River, and on his death in 1634 his son Erdeni Batur took the title of khong taiji and united most of the remaining Oirats.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
